    description:
      "The 2-in-1 format raises a folding handrail out of the deck, which changes what the machine can do. With the rail up it will run to 6 mph, so it doubles as a light jogging treadmill in a way none of the flat pads can; with the rail down it slides under a desk like the rest. The cost of that flexibility is bulk. It is thicker and heavier than a true pad, so it stays where you put it rather than disappearing under a sofa, and the deck cushioning is thin enough that longer sessions can become uncomfortable. If you want one machine rather than two, this is the compromise; if you want a device that vanishes, it is not.",
  },
  {
    id: "goyouth-2-in-1",
    name: "GOYOUTH 2-in-1",
    editorialScore: 4.0,
    category: "Best for Shared Households",
    badge: "Quiet Pick",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=GOYOUTH+2+in+1+Under+Desk+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    priceRange: "$299-$399",
    specs: "0.6-4.0 mph · 42 in. belt · folding handrail · 240 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "Shock-absorbing layer should help keep footfall noise down",
      "Shock-absorbing deck layer is unusual at this price",
      "Handrail folds flat for pure under-desk use",
      "Low price leaves room in the budget for a proper mat",
    ],
    cons: [
      "240 lb capacity is lower than the Sunny and Urevo",
      "4.0 mph ceiling is well below the Sunny's 6 mph",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pick for open-plan homes and shared offices where noise matters most.",
    description:
      "Noise is one of the most common reasons people stop using a pad mid-workday, and the GOYOUTH is our pick for that problem. We have not measured it ourselves; the reason it stands out is the shock-absorbing layer under the belt, which is unusual at this price: less impact into the deck generally means less noise out of it, and less into the floor. The trade-offs are minor rather than absent. Its 240 lb capacity is lower than the Sunny's and the Urevo's, and its 4.0 mph ceiling rules out the light jogging the Sunny allows. For a quiet desk walker, neither is likely to matter.",
