                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold mb-4">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                    <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm inline-block">
                      Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
                    </a>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="desk-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Getting the Desk Setup Right</h2>
          <p>
            The pad is half the purchase. The other half is the desk, and
            people who skip this part are the ones who develop shoulder or
            wrist complaints and blame the treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            Standing on a pad raises you by two to three inches, so your
            desk needs to be that much higher than a normal standing height. A
            fixed-height standing desk that was correct for you before will be
            slightly too low afterwards, which pushes your shoulders down and
            your wrists into extension. An electric sit-stand desk solves this
            in seconds; a fixed desk may need risers. Set the surface so your
            forearms are level while standing on the pad, not beside it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Monitor height moves with you. Raise the screen so the top edge sits
            at eye level from your new standing height, because looking down
            while walking is the fastest route to neck strain. If you use a
            laptop alone, this setup will not work for long — a separate
            keyboard and a raised screen are close to essential once you are
            walking for hours rather than minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, leave clear space behind the pad. Belt speed is low, but
            people do step backwards off a walking pad, and having a metre of
            open floor behind you is the difference between an awkward step and
            a fall. That is doubly true if you have the machine on a rug or up
            against furniture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Speed to Actually Walk At</h2>
          <p>
            We haven't run typing tests ourselves, but the general pattern is consistent and has nothing to do with the brand of pad. At very slow speeds most people type about as well as when seated. As speed rises, vertical head movement increases, and around 2.5 mph longer typing passages become noticeably harder; by 3 mph careful writing is difficult for most people.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical answer is to keep a slow, steady pace for focused work and save higher speeds for calls, reading and meetings. That may
            feel too slow to be worthwhile; it is not. Four hours at 2 mph is
            roughly eight miles of walking that would otherwise not have
            happened, and the point of a pad is volume rather than intensity.
            If you find yourself wanting to push the speed up, that is a signal
            you want a workout rather than a step count, and a workout wants a
            different machine. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmill workouts</a>
            covers what that would look like, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills and weight loss</a>
            makes the case that accumulated volume of exactly this kind is worth
            more than most people assume.
          </p>
          <p>
            Build up gradually. Thirty minutes on day one, an hour by the end of
            week one, and only then longer stretches. Calves and the arches of
            the feet are the parts that complain when people go from a seated
            day to four hours of walking overnight, and a week of soreness is
            enough to end the habit before it forms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Noise, Floors and Neighbours</h2>
          <p>
            Airborne noise from a good pad at 2 mph is low — below a
            dishwasher, and easily covered by a headset. That is not what causes
            complaints. The problem is structure-borne noise: each footfall
            passes through a deck with almost no cushioning, into the floor, and
            into the room or flat below as a rhythmic thud. Pads are worse at
            this than full treadmills precisely because they are thin.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fix that works is mass and damping directly under the machine: a
            dense rubber mat, ideally 6mm or thicker, sized to the whole
            footprint. Foam gym tiles are noticeably less effective — they
            compress and then transmit. On a suspended timber floor, position
            the pad near a load-bearing wall rather than in the middle of a
            span, where the floor flexes most. The physics here is the same as
            for a full-size machine, and we go through it in more depth in our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are in an apartment with people below, the honest advice is
            to test before you commit to a routine: run the pad for ten minutes
            and ask your neighbour. It is a five-minute conversation that avoids
            a long-running problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Lifespan and Maintenance</h2>
          <p>
            A walking pad used four hours a day is running a duty cycle no
            treadmill motor of that size was designed around, and that is the
            single biggest determinant of how long it lasts. Two to four years
            is the realistic window under heavy use, longer if the machine only
            runs an hour a day.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two maintenance jobs extend that meaningfully. The first is belt
            lubrication: most pads take silicone oil under the belt every three
            to six months, and a dry belt is the most common cause of a motor
            burning out early. The second is belt tracking — if the belt drifts
            towards one side, the adjustment bolts at the rear correct it, and
            leaving it to rub against the frame will destroy the belt edge
            within weeks. Both take five minutes and both are in the manual that
            most owners never open.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also keep the machine off carpet if you can. Carpet fibres get drawn
            into the motor housing and the rollers, and it is the fastest way we
            know to shorten a pad's life. A hard mat over carpet is fine; the
            carpet directly under the deck is not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Final Recommendations</h2>
          <p>
            The <strong>WalkingPad P1</strong> is the machine we would buy. It is built for the speed band that matters, it folds in half so it
            genuinely gets put away, and the speed steps are fine enough to find
            your working pace. Nothing else got the fundamentals as right.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are unsure the habit will stick, the
            <strong>Urevo SpaceWalk 3S</strong> costs little enough to find out
            with, as long as you are not tall. In an open-plan home the <strong>GOYOUTH 2-in-1</strong> is our pick for noise. In a
            very small space, or for a shorter user who wants some incline, the
            <strong>Egofit Walker Pro</strong> is the specialist. And if this
            has to be your only machine, the <strong>Sunny SF-T7971</strong>
            handles both jobs at the cost of never quite disappearing.
          </p>
          <p>
            If reading this has convinced you the pad is not enough machine, that
            is a useful outcome. Take that decision properly in
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs. treadmill</a>
            before you spend anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are under-desk treadmills actually worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it for one specific job: adding several thousand
                low-intensity steps to a working day you would otherwise spend
                seated. Judged as a fitness machine they are poor, because they
                have no incline, short decks and thin cushioning. Judged as a
                way to stop sitting for eight hours, they work, and the people
                who keep using them are the ones who bought them for that
                reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you type while walking on an under-desk treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, below roughly 2.5 mph. Above that, vertical head movement makes reading harder and typing accuracy drops noticeably for most people. The usual approach is to keep a slow, steady pace for focused work, and use the higher speeds only for calls, reading or meetings where you are not typing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is a walking pad in an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At 2 mph a good walking pad is quieter than most dishwashers in
                the air, but airborne noise is not the problem in an apartment.
                Footfall passes through the thin deck into the floor and carries
                to the flat below. A dense rubber mat underneath is the single
                most effective fix, and it matters more on a walking pad than on
                a full treadmill because pads have far less cushioning of their
                own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do walking pads last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two to four years of daily use is a realistic expectation,
                against eight to twelve for a well-built full-size treadmill.
                Pads use smaller motors running at a high duty cycle, thinner
                decks and lighter rollers, and most carry warranties of one to
                two years that reflect this. Budget accordingly rather than
                treating a pad as a decade-long purchase.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do walking pads have incline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost none do. A small number offer a fixed incline of around
                5%, and the Egofit Walker Pro in this guide is one of them, but
                adjustable incline is essentially unavailable in the category.
                If incline walking is your goal, you want a full-size treadmill
                instead — this is the clearest single dividing line between the
                two formats.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What speed should you walk at while working?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start at 1.5 mph for the first week and settle wherever your typing accuracy holds up, which for most people is a little below the point where typing starts to suffer. The aim is a pace you can forget about. Anything fast enough to demand attention will compete with the work, and the habit tends to collapse within a fortnight.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Under-Desk Treadmills</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These four account for nearly every abandoned walking pad we hear about. None of them is the machine's fault.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a pad when you wanted a treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If your goal is a workout with progression, a pad will disappoint you within a month: no incline, a short belt and thin cushioning leave nowhere to go. Pads are for step volume during work. Decide which of the two you actually want before you order, and if it is a workout, look at full-size walking treadmills instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring belt length against your height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Belts here span 34 to 47 inches, and the difference is decisive rather than marginal. Users over 6 feet are likely to find the 40-inch belts constraining and the 34-inch model unusable. Treat the belt length as a hard filter against your height, not as a spec to trade away for a lower price.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the desk at its old height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Standing on a pad lifts you two to three inches, so a standing desk that was set correctly is now too low. Working like that for hours drops your shoulders and bends your wrists back, and the resulting ache gets blamed on the treadmill. Re-set the desk so your forearms are level while you are standing on the machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the mat and the lubrication</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A dense rubber mat is what keeps footfall out of your neighbour's ceiling, and silicone lubricant under the belt every three to six months is what keeps a small motor alive. Both are cheap and both are routinely skipped. The complaints that follow — noise downstairs, a belt that slips, a motor that dies in year two — are almost always these two omissions.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

